# Inside Mobius: the process tree

## Introduction

[Mobius](https://github.com/mobius-home/mobius) is a small library that collects
[`telemetry`](https://hexdocs.pm/telemetry) metrics locally on a device and keeps
a rolling history you can plot right on the device. It is a handful of cooperating
processes, and the best way to understand how it works is to *look* at them.

This notebook uses [`Kino.Process`](https://kino.hexdocs.pm/Kino.Process.html) to
draw Mobius' supervision tree and explain what each process does. A companion
notebook, **Watching Mobius work**, traces the messages that flow between these
processes while Mobius answers a query.

Nothing here changes any state, so it is safe to run against a live system.

## Find the running Mobius processes

Rather than hard-code names, we discover Mobius by scanning the process registry.
Mobius registers each worker as `Mobius.<Component>.<instance>` (for example
`Mobius.Scraper.mobius`) and its supervisor as `Mobius.Supervisor`. Let's list every
registered name under the `Mobius.` namespace.

```elixir
mobius_names =
  Process.registered()
  |> Enum.filter(&String.starts_with?(Atom.to_string(&1), "Elixir.Mobius."))
  |> Enum.sort()

Kino.DataTable.new(Enum.map(mobius_names, &%{process: inspect(&1)}))
```

The *instance* (usually `:mobius`) is the lowercase tail on a worker's name. We read
it off a worker rather than the supervisor, since the supervisor name carries no
instance suffix. If this raises, Mobius is not started yet: add
`{Mobius, metrics: [...]}` to your supervision tree and re-run.

```elixir
instance =
  Enum.find_value(mobius_names, fn name ->
    case Atom.to_string(name) do
      "Elixir.Mobius.Scraper." <> instance -> instance
      "Elixir.Mobius.Registry." <> instance -> instance
      "Elixir.Mobius.ReportServer." <> instance -> instance
      _ -> nil
    end
  end)

instance =
  case instance do
    nil -> raise "No Mobius worker processes are running. Start one with {Mobius, metrics: [...]}."
    name -> String.to_atom(name)
  end
```

And the supervisor (what we will draw the tree from). We match the `Mobius.Supervisor`
name with or without an instance suffix, so this works across Mobius versions.

```elixir
supervisor =
  Enum.find(mobius_names, fn name ->
    string = Atom.to_string(name)
    string == "Elixir.Mobius.Supervisor" or String.starts_with?(string, "Elixir.Mobius.Supervisor.")
  end)
```

## The supervision tree

This is the heart of Mobius. `Kino.Process.sup_tree/2` walks the supervisor and
renders every child. Solid lines are supervisor/worker links; dotted lines are
plain process links.

```elixir
Kino.Process.sup_tree(supervisor, direction: :left_right)
```

Each worker has a single job:

| Process | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `Mobius.Registry` | Attaches the `:telemetry` handlers for your metrics and events when Mobius starts. |
| `Mobius.Scraper` | Once a second, snapshots the current metric values into an in-memory round-robin database. This is the history you plot. |
| `Mobius.ReportServer` | Answers "what is new since I last asked" queries (`get_latest_metrics/1`, `get_latest_events/1`). |
| `Mobius.EventsServer` | Keeps the event log, a ring buffer of discrete events. |
| `Mobius.TimeServer` | Tracks when Mobius started and, on devices that boot without a real clock, corrects event timestamps once the clock syncs. |
| `Mobius.MetricsTable.Monitor` | Writes the current-values table to disk on a graceful shutdown so history survives a reboot. |

The current metric *values* themselves live in an [ETS](https://www.erlang.org/doc/man/ets)
table owned by the supervisor, not in a process of their own. Telemetry handlers
write straight into that table, which is why recording a metric is cheap. The
Scraper periodically copies that table into the history database.

## Where Mobius sits in the larger picture

`Kino.Process.app_tree/2` shows an entire OTP application's tree. Mobius runs under
whichever application started it, so on a Nerves Livebook device that is usually
`:nerves_livebook`. Look for the `Mobius.Supervisor.*` subtree among the other
children.

```elixir
Kino.Process.app_tree(:nerves_livebook, direction: :left_right)
```

If you started Mobius somewhere else, swap in that application's name. You can list
running applications with `Application.started_applications/0`.

## What is Mobius actually tracking?

Now that the processes make sense, peek at their contents. `Mobius.Registry.metrics/1`
returns the `Telemetry.Metrics` definitions the Registry attached handlers for.

```elixir
Mobius.Registry.metrics(instance)
```

`Mobius.info/1` prints the current value of every metric straight from the ETS
table the Scraper reads.

```elixir
Mobius.info(instance)
```

